<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmed Hassan al-Bakr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1914–1982</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmed Hassan al-Bakr was an Iraqi army officer and Ba'athist politician who was President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1968 to 1979. Briefly prime minister after the Ba'athist coup of 1963, he led the party back to power in July 1968 and ruled with his relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saddam-hussein">Saddam Hussein</a> as deputy. His government nationalised the Iraq Petroleum Company in 1972; in 1979 he resigned, officially on health grounds, and Saddam succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-hassan-al-bakr.jpg" alt="Ahmed Hassan al-Bakr" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_Hassan_el_Bakr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
